#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# disk

#% text
# `disk([x, y], r)` is a graphics primitive: a filled disk of radius r centred at [x, y].

#% text
# `disk([x, y], [rx, ry])` is a filled ellipse with semi-axes rx and ry along the coordinate axes.

#% text
# `disk([x, y], r, [a0, a1])` is a sector from angle a0 to angle a1, in radians, anticlockwise.

#% text
# `disk()` is the unit disk at the origin.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The disk is filled in the current colour, or as `face_form` says; its edge is drawn only after an `edge_form` directive.

#% item
# Radii are in plot coordinates, so a disk looks round only when the two axes have equal units (the default `aspect_ratio: automatic`).

#% item
# With numbers for the centre and radius the arguments are checked: a negative radius gives a failure naming the argument. With symbols the term stays as it is.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A disk in the default colour:

#% cell
graphics(disk([0, 0], 1))
#% output
#= image("disk.files/disk-1.svgz")

#% text
# A red ellipse beside a quarter of a blue disk:

#% cell
graphics([red, disk([0, 0], [2, 1]), blue, disk([3, 0], 1, [0, pi/2])])
#% output
#= image("disk.files/disk-2.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A translucent disk with a black edge over a gray square:

#% cell
graphics([gray, rectangle([-1, -1], [1, 1]), opacity(0.6), orange, edge_form(black), disk([0.5, 0.5], 1)], frame: true)
#% output
#= image("disk.files/disk-3.svgz")

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A negative radius is an error:

#% cell
disk([0, 0], -1)
#% output
#! failure(argument, disk, 2)

#% text
# A symbolic radius leaves the primitive as it is; it can be drawn once the radius has a value:

#% cell
disk([0, 0], r)
#% output
#= disk([0, 0], r)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `circle`, `rectangle`, `polygon`, `face_form`, `edge_form`, `graphics`
